# Security rules

## Secrets
- Real secrets only in `.env` (gitignored). Templates = `*.env.example` (names,
  placeholders). Never commit `.env`, `*.key`, `*.pem`, `*.db`, `credentials*`.
- If a secret is exposed: rotate it at the provider, `git rm --cached`, re-scan.

## Application security
- Validate and sanitize all external input (uploads, filenames, owner names,
  spreadsheet cells, API responses). Filenames must be path-traversal-safe
  (`backend/server.py:sanitize_filename`).
- Bound resource use: cap upload size (`MAX_UPLOAD_MB`), cap loops/pagination.
- HTTP calls must set explicit timeouts. Avoid SSRF: don't fetch user-supplied
  URLs without allow-listing.
- DB access must use parameterized queries (never string-formatted SQL).
- CORS: configure via `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGINS`; never wildcard + credentials.
- Never log secrets or full LLM responses containing PII; redact.
- Catch specific exceptions; never bare `except:`. Don't leak internals in API
  error messages.

## Dependencies & CI
- Keep stdlib modules (e.g. `sqlite3`) out of requirements files.
- Add least-privilege `permissions:` to every GitHub workflow. Treat PR/issue
  text and branch names as untrusted. Pin third-party actions by SHA.
- Run `python scripts/security_scan.py` before finishing security-relevant work.
